--TEST--
`Core\Cli\Text::plain` passes `LF` and `TAB` through and shows every other control byte — ADR 0086 § 1
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 1's table is a substitution and not a deletion, and the bound has two
// sides. `LF` and `TAB` are layout rather than commands, so a carrier built
// from a whole line keeps its shape and a program can still lay a table out.
var $tab = Core\Str::fromCodePoint(9);
var $lf = Core\Str::fromCodePoint(10);
echo Core\Cli\Text::plain("a" . $tab . "b" . $lf);

// The other side: a bare `CR` and a `DEL` are commands a terminal consumes and
// shows to nobody, so each comes out as its own visible glyph instead.
var $cr = Core\Str::fromCodePoint(13);
var $del = Core\Str::fromCodePoint(127);
echo Core\Cli\Text::plain("c" . $cr . $del), "\n";
--EXPECT--
a	b
c␍␡
